service similar to a lot

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:06.910
of the other ones out
there, but right now we

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.625
focus mostly on open source
and on premise and enterprise.

00:07:10.625 --> 00:07:13.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, and
before we get into the details

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.627
on how it is implemented,
what's the name Drone?

00:07:16.627 --> 00:07:18.460
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: The
idea with the name Drone

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:21.700
came from this idea
that it was automated,

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:23.860
that you had something
controlling and executing

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.750
your builds in an
automated fashion,

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:30.100
from a central source
like that Borg source.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.741
I think that's a name you guys
are familiar with at Google.

00:07:32.741 --> 00:07:33.657
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Borg?

00:07:33.657 --> 00:07:35.717
Yeah, maybe.

00:07:35.717 --> 00:07:37.800
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: The name
came like five years ago,

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.091
maybe when it was a little
less of a controversial term

00:07:40.091 --> 00:07:41.590
and then it just kind of stuck.

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.780
And domain names
are hard to come by,

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.830
so I just wasn't interested
in changing at that point.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:49.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so
let's talk a little bit more

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.260
about exactly how Drone works.

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.920
Like you said, it's
an open source project

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.690
so you can run it anywhere.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:57.430
Where are people running it?

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:58.630
How do you run it?

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:01.390
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Sure,
well drone is built in Go,

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:04.990
so it compiles down to
a nice small binary that

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.570
has no external
runtime dependencies.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:10.360
It ships in like a 15
megabyte Docker scratch

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:14.824
image, so it's really
easy to get up and running

00:08:14.824 --> 00:08:16.240
and you can really
run it anywhere

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:21.490
you want on any cloud
within your data center

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:23.980
on your own infrastructure.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:25.520
It's a really portable system.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.478
MARK MANDEL: Now, I think
we were talking about

00:08:27.478 --> 00:08:29.609
as well before the episode--

00:08:29.609 --> 00:08:31.900
most people run it on Kubernetes
or a lot of people do?

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:34.330
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Yeah, I mean,
I think it's incredible.

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:38.590
I remember a year or two ago,
Kubernetes came up somewhat

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:39.250
frequently.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:41.590
I think now, within
the drone community,

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:45.700
almost every day someone is
installing Drone on Kubernetes.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:48.760
I'm blown away and I
think, as a result,

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.470
we're spending a
lot more time trying

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:54.550
to integrate with Kubernetes
and make that a more

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:55.854
seamless experience.

00:08:55.854 --> 00:08:58.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, could
you tell us a little bit more

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:00.100
about, why Kubernetes?

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:04.035
Maybe Jessie knows about this?

00:09:04.035 --> 00:09:05.410
Like, why do you
think Kubernetes

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:08.594
would be a good environment
to run such a thing as Drone.

00:09:08.594 --> 00:09:10.510
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah,
I think a lot of people

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:14.300
actually use Kubernetes,
most of the time, for CI

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.270
processes because you
have a bunch of jobs,

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:18.274
you don't really care
on which nodes they run,

00:09:18.274 --> 00:09:19.690
you just need to
get it somewhere.

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:21.410
And it needs to complete.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.610
So having the basic
scheduler there

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:28.840
to handle a bunch of jobs for
your corporation or something

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:29.509
is super nice.

00:09:29.509 --> 00:09:31.300
MARK MANDEL: I'm sure
the isolation is also

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:32.323
a nice factor, too.

00:09:32.323 --> 00:09:33.781
JESSIE FRAZELLE:
Oh yeah, of course

00:09:33.781 --> 00:09:35.350
because containers
isolate and then

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:37.990
you kind of have peace of
mind that team two is not

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.540
messing with team one.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:42.640
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So, whenever I think

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:45.810
about a bunch of different
tasks that need to be processed,

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:49.030
especially for Kubernetes, the
first thing that comes to mind

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:51.370
is the Jobs API.

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:53.080
Are you using it?

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:54.500
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: So, right now--

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.890
we initially started
looking at the Jobs API.

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.170
And one of the
suggestions we got

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.540
was, instead of
using the jobs API,

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.560
to create custom
namespaces, one for each

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.040
build and then
inside that namespace

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.450
spin up pods with
persistent volumes.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.019
And then, for each step in the
pipeline creating a new pod.

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:14.310
MARK MANDEL: So, for those
people who aren't familiar--

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:15.690
and I'll defer to you, Jessie--

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:16.837
what's a namespace?

00:10:16.837 --> 00:10:17.670
What does that mean?

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.170
JESSIE FRAZELLE:
Yeah, so Kubernetes

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.770
has this concept of namespaces
where you can almost segregate

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:26.220
how your cluster is defined
in terms of where things are

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:30.330
running and how they run
in terms of interactions

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:31.406
with other containers.

00:10:31.406 --> 00:10:33.030
So when you namespace
something, you're

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:35.220
blocking off network
traffic, stuff like that

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.080
from interacting with
another namespace.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.390
And where we're even
getting to in the future

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:43.200
is where your security
model could be the namespace

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.270
as a boundary, which is
why it would actually

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:47.550
be great for Drone,
in this case,

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:50.430
because then maybe even
multitenant jobs not

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:54.990
in the same company could be
run on Kubernetes as a CI, which

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:56.080
would be really cool.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.538
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So, basically, you

00:10:57.538 --> 00:11:01.770
could say that it's one
more layer of isolation,

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:05.280
like, containers provide
a layer of isolation,

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.380
namespaces is like
a second one on top.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:10.560
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah, it's
just the more abstract layer

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:11.820
in terms of--

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:17.490
instead of isolation from
system calls of Linux

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:19.820
and stuff like that, you're
just one more layer up

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:21.750
of it's isolating the
parts of Kubernetes

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:23.150
that you don't want to interact.

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:24.450
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: I think
the interesting thing, too,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.730
about namespaces, you can
correct me if I'm wrong,

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:29.430
Jessie, but my understanding is
if you delete the namespace it

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:30.600
cleans everything up.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:31.915
So, from a CI perspective--

00:11:31.915 --> 00:11:32.277
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that's nice.

00:11:32.277 --> 00:11:32.970
BRAD RYDZEWSKI:
--it's really nice

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:35.520
because you don't have to worry
about-- a pipeline might have

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.480
10 or 15 steps all creating
and spawning containers,

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:42.277
so the fact that you can
just, with one API call,

00:11:42.277 --> 00:11:44.860
wipe all that out and not worry
about leaving artifacts around

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.760
on the system is really nice,
at least from an integration

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:48.330
standpoint.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:49.410
MARK MANDEL: Probably
also means you're

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:51.450
less likely to accidentally
wipe off something

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:52.874
you shouldn't be wiping off.

00:11:52.874 --> 00:11:53.790
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:54.780
That's genius, actually.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:56.040
I didn't think of
that as a feature.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:57.570
It's like a hidden
feature, there.

00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:58.440
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Yes.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:11:59.310
MARK MANDEL: I didn't
know that was the thing.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:00.630
I'm actually now really
excited about it.

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:01.240
That's cool.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:02.781
BRAD RYDZEWSKI:
Credit goes to Kelsey

00:12:02.781 --> 00:12:04.680
because he's the one
that told me that that's

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:05.780
what I should be doing.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.321
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Dear coworker
Kelsey [? Hightower, ?] hello.

00:12:08.321 --> 00:12:10.650
MARK MANDEL: Excellent, so
now I'm also just curious.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:13.504
You said that for
using Drone you

00:12:13.504 --> 00:12:14.670
write a Docker Compose file.

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:18.470
What is the developer experience
like when they use Drone?

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:20.532
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Sure,
I mean, Drone supports

00:12:20.532 --> 00:12:21.990
a lot of different
workflows, but I

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:26.130
think the most interesting
is Drone uses, like I said,

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:29.250
a superset of Docker Compose,
and in many ways behaves

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:30.090
like Docker Compose.

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:32.490
You can actually run
your build locally

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:33.990
just by running
drone exec and it'll

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:35.850
spin up-- let's say
you're using Docker

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.580
for Mac-- it'll spin up and
run your build in isolation,

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:39.510
locally.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.640
So, gone are the
days where you're

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:44.580
pushing to your CI
server, seeing it fail,

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:47.790
updating your YAML, pushing
in kind of rinse and repeat,

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:49.815
doing that over and
over and banging

00:12:49.815 --> 00:12:50.940
your head against the wall.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I've done that.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:54.358
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: So, yeah,
I think it starts with that

00:12:54.358 --> 00:12:58.434
and a lot of individuals
really like that workflow.

00:12:58.434 --> 00:12:59.850
So then when you
push to something

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:03.540
like GitHub or
Bitbucket or GitLab,

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.760
Drone supports many
different providers.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.160
Right now, everything
gets enqueued,

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:09.990
and Drone will pull
down the builds

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:13.260
onto one or many
machines and execute

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:15.570
the build by
interacting directly

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:19.620
with the Docker host
spinning up those containers.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.020
And so the nice thing is you
have a level of determinism

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:25.110
because the build that you
executed from the command line

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:28.110
on your machine runs
the exact same way

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:30.260
in your CI environment
on your servers.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:31.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:34.650
I think it's time to go a little
bit more into the architecture.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.590
You mentioned that it
is running Go-- yay!

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:39.420
You also mentioned
that it was basically

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.220
just like a Docker container.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:43.530
Everything was
running on containers,

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:46.470
you can run it very
easily on Kubernetes.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.810
What are the other
dependencies that you have?

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:50.890
Is there any other
dependencies-- like,

00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:52.810
what do you do for
storage or communication

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:53.636
across containers?

00:13:53.636 --> 00:13:54.510
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Sure.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:57.360
So in terms of dependencies,
the most important thing,

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:58.860
in my opinion, with
Drone is that it

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:02.280
ships as a single binary with
no hard, external dependencies

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.697
other than a container runtime.

00:14:03.697 --> 00:14:05.280
And so that's important
because no one

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.909
wants to download and
install open source software

00:14:07.909 --> 00:14:10.200
and then have to install 10
other pieces of open source

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:12.240
software.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:17.280
But that being said,
Go makes it really easy

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:19.680
to do abstractions
with interfaces.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:23.310
One of the goals is to ship
Drone with built-in modules

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:26.190
like a queue, like Pub/Sub,
like log streaming,

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:28.831
but to allow you to swap
those out with something-

00:14:28.831 --> 00:14:31.080
and I'm going to use this
buzzword word, but something

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:32.640
more Cloud-native.

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.770
So, yeah, I know, right?

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.700
Everyone's laughing,
but I think that's--

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:40.110
and it's one of those terms
that's thrown around a lot.

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:42.026
And it's kind of lost a
little bit of meaning.

00:14:42.026 --> 00:14:44.700
But for me, what I
want with something

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:49.320
that's considered Cloud-native
is you install the software,

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:51.300
you're running it, but
you can swap out modules

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:52.710
with things that are
going to scale that you

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:54.090
don't have to worry about.

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:59.610
So for example, Drone ships with
an internal in-memory queue.

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:02.010
But, using Go and
using interfaces,

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:05.730
you can swap it out very easily
with Google Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:06.951
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:15:06.951 --> 00:15:09.200
MARK MANDEL: So I was going
to ask, you said using Go,

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.700
using interfaces--

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:13.430
is that using the Go
1.8 plugin system?

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:15.350
Or how are you
actually switching out

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:16.380
from one to the other?

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:20.270
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: So right now
we provide those baked in.

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:21.770
So right now if
you're using Drone,

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:24.860
you can use the in-memory queue
or the Cloud Pub/Sub queue.

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:26.640
Those are the only two we offer.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:29.452
Maybe we'll look at the plugins.

00:15:29.452 --> 00:15:31.160
It's a great and
interesting new feature.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:32.930
So it's something we'll
definitely look into.

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:34.790
MARK MANDEL: Are you configuring
that through an environment

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:35.930
variable, or is
that how it works?

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:36.390
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Yep.

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:38.098
You just flip a few
environment variables

00:15:38.098 --> 00:15:39.530
and then you're good to go.

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:44.210
And so then we can do that with
other modules in terms of you

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:46.610
got the queue, now
you have logging.

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:48.470
Drone has an in-memory
log multiplexer.

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:51.290
You can use stack
driver logging.

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:52.850
You can do that
with the EventBus.

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:55.250
You can use Pub/Sub
and another goal

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:57.200
is maybe using Kubernetes
as the back end

00:15:57.200 --> 00:16:00.410
instead of in memory
or a local Docker.

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:02.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:06.710
And once you're able to
basically break that--

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:10.550
I'm going to use the
word monolith, that

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:14.420
monolith you mentioned that you
have one single static binary

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.794
build with Go into
more pieces, I

00:16:17.794 --> 00:16:19.710
guess that you're also
thinking about the fact

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:23.000
that you can scale some of the
pieces independent of the rest?

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.300
What are your plans on that?

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:26.550
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Yeah, I mean,
I think that's the goal,

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:29.090
is letting the developer
pick and choose

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:33.140
what they want to scale
or maybe, as Drone grows,

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:35.840
it can kind of grow with them
by leveraging Google Cloud

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:38.330
and the services it provides.

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:41.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm curious
about Jesse's opinion

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:44.240
on the definition
of Cloud-native

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:46.790
because I know that she
has opinions on this.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:48.940
So what is Cloud-native for you?

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:50.690
JESSIE FRAZELLE: I was
actually just going

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:52.481
to joke that if you
say that one more time,

00:16:52.481 --> 00:16:54.170
Eric Brewer is going
to show up here.

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:54.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cloud-native.

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:55.060
Hello, Eric.

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:56.456
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:57.542 --> 00:16:59.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: He's probably
somewhere around here.

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:03.440
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah,
it's such a buzzword.

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:06.290
I think that it's just honestly
anything that you can deploy

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:10.520
in the cloud easily,
which is a lot of things--

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:13.250
and I think people just
kind of throw that around--

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:15.200
but also things that
are built into tools

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:19.040
that are the cloud like using
Pub/Sub, using integrations

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:22.880
into Google Cloud itself
is super Cloud-native.

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:25.220
I feel like a
salesperson right now.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:26.220
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: I agree.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.069
I think we need to take
it one step further.

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:29.685
I think that's
what Cloud-native--

00:17:29.685 --> 00:17:31.946
I said it one more time, sorry.

00:17:31.946 --> 00:17:33.320
I think that's
what it should be,

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:34.910
I think it's all
about integrating

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:38.720
with those built-in
services that Google Cloud

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:41.450
Platform or maybe
other providers

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:44.210
have to offer to give you
that extra scalability

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:46.489
and maintainability,
and I wish we

00:17:46.489 --> 00:17:47.780
were more strict with the term.

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:49.390
So that's kind of how I use it.

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:51.723
MARK MANDEL: Do you look at
it also from the perspective

00:17:51.723 --> 00:17:53.440
of, it's elastic, right?

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:55.190
If you build certain
apps in certain ways,

00:17:55.190 --> 00:17:57.273
they're very hard to scale
because they might have

00:17:57.273 --> 00:17:58.520
dependencies on other things.

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:00.950
We look at things like 12
Factor Apps, that kind of stuff.

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:03.033
I think of that as well
when I think Cloud-native,

00:18:03.033 --> 00:18:05.870
like I've built my app such
that, if it lives in the Cloud,

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:08.510
I can have 1,000 servers, I can
have 2000, I could have 10,000,

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:09.600
it doesn't matter.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:11.600
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah,
and the scalability of it

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:13.433
and the fact that you
can tear down, spin it

00:18:13.433 --> 00:18:16.120
up, a lot of that, I think,
comes from containers.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:20.600
But being able to just clean
slate, just start from nothing

00:18:20.600 --> 00:18:23.690
and go from everything is huge.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:26.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, last time
we talked about Drone here

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:28.260
on the podcast
was because it was

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:32.990
a cool thing of the week, which
was because the New York Times,

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:34.340
they created--

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:38.990
is it a plugin for Drone so
you can deploy stuff directly

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:41.540
to App Engine, container engine?

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:42.780
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Correct.

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:45.140
Yeah, so Drone has a
really nice plugin system

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:48.380
where every step in the
pipeline is a container

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:50.840
and that includes
plugins themselves.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:55.040
So writing plugins is as
simple as putting a Bash script

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:59.050
in a container so they
can be really any language

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:01.010
and there's a really
low barrier to entry.

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:02.780
So the New York Times
created a number

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:05.261
of plugins including
Kubernetes plugins.

00:19:05.261 --> 00:19:07.010
And Drone actually has
probably about five

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:09.690
or six different
Kubernetes plugins that

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:12.680
would work with Kubernetes
in different ways,

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:15.500
some more simple and
some more advanced.

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:17.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, what is
the whole idea behind this?

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:20.780
It means that I can basically
have my YAML file that you were

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:23.330
mentioning at the beginning
and at some point just say,

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:25.940
whenever there's a
new change in my repo,

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:28.400
this is going to
call Drone, it's

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:30.920
going to create a new
image, new deployment.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:32.170
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Yeah, correct.

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:36.699
So we have plugins for
Google Container Registry.

00:19:36.699 --> 00:19:38.240
So in the case of
the New York Times,

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:40.655
they were pushing to Google
Container Registry They

00:19:40.655 --> 00:19:42.450
had a simple plugin to do that.

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:44.990
And then, once it
was in the registry,

00:19:44.990 --> 00:19:48.470
they used the Kubernetes
plugin and a spec file,

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:50.720
and they used that
to automatically push

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.660
the new image to Kubernetes
and auto-deploy it

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:56.060
to a number of
different instances.

00:19:56.060 --> 00:19:59.720
And I've even seen
teams where they

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:02.375
were deploying pool requests.

00:20:02.375 --> 00:20:05.207
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice,
to smoke test it.

00:20:05.207 --> 00:20:06.790
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: Yeah,
to smoke test it

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:08.915
and so you do some really
interesting things there.

00:20:08.915 --> 00:20:11.020
There's a lot of
interesting options.

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:12.979
MARK MANDEL: Well, from
those plugins like that

00:20:12.979 --> 00:20:15.394
and you were talking about
having the Pub/Sub integration,

00:20:15.394 --> 00:20:16.780
are there any
plans going forward

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:19.340
to be more integrated with
Google Cloud Platform?

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:20.650
What's the plans there?

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:23.320
BRAD RYDZEWSKI: I think ideally,
all those different modules

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:25.990
we talked about, which is
queueing, which is eventing,

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:28.690
logging, those are all
common with a CI system.

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:30.280
You'll find them
in any CI system

