00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:01.480
His name is Rokesh,
Rokesh Jankie.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:03.590
I hope I pronounced
your name correctly,

00:25:03.590 --> 00:25:05.590
unlike you who misspelled
your name in the email

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:08.260
you sent me, but anyway--

00:25:08.260 --> 00:25:11.980
so his question is, OK, so what
is protocol buffers and why

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:13.490
should we all start using it?

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:14.989
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
what is protobuf?

00:25:14.989 --> 00:25:17.780
So, it is another open source
project that came from Google.

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:19.530
It's actually been
around for a long time.

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:22.840
What you can basically take
from it is it's an IDL.

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:24.440
It's an Interface
Definition Language.

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:26.386
So you write these protofiles.

00:25:26.386 --> 00:25:27.760
And from those
protofiles, you're

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:30.100
able to basically
take the data that

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:31.990
is defined in those
protofiles and send it

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.330
across the wire in a variety
of different languages.

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:36.454
And it's a binary format
that goes across the wire.

00:25:36.454 --> 00:25:39.530
So it's nice and small,
very performant, and super,

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:40.480
super cool that way.

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:42.190
The nice thing is
it's contract first,

00:25:42.190 --> 00:25:44.440
so you're writing this
Interface Definition Language.

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:46.773
Your sender and your receiver
both have an understanding

00:25:46.773 --> 00:25:49.177
of exactly the data they should
be getting and receiving.

00:25:49.177 --> 00:25:50.260
So that's hugely powerful.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:51.900
But it also gives you nice
things like versioning.

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:53.410
So if your data
changes, you don't

00:25:53.410 --> 00:25:55.900
have to worry about it as much
between sender and receiver,

00:25:55.900 --> 00:25:57.350
and do all sorts of
fun stuff like that.

00:25:57.350 --> 00:25:58.808
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, just to give

00:25:58.808 --> 00:26:00.580
an idea of how
important this thing is,

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:03.562
we use it at Google for
virtually everything.

00:26:03.562 --> 00:26:04.270
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:06.519
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Whatever
we encode anything in files,

00:26:06.519 --> 00:26:07.810
it's always protocol buffers.

00:26:07.810 --> 00:26:09.790
Wherever we sending
data over the network,

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:11.260
it's always protocol buffers.

00:26:11.260 --> 00:26:15.675
So it is, basically, we do not
use JSON internally that much.

00:26:15.675 --> 00:26:17.050
Instead, we use
protocol buffers.

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:20.650
So you can see it like a
more efficient and easier

00:26:20.650 --> 00:26:22.602
to collaborate way of
sending data around.

00:26:22.602 --> 00:26:25.060
MARK MANDEL: If you're going
to play with protocol buffers,

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:27.310
I would definitely say have
a look at the gRPC project.

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:28.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:30.416
MARK MANDEL: It is the default
serialization and basically

00:26:30.416 --> 00:26:32.830
core interface to gRPC.

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:35.140
We have two episodes that
we've talked about it on.

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:39.186
Episode number 15, we had Varun
Talwar, who is the PM on it.

00:26:39.186 --> 00:26:40.810
And we also talked
with Brandon Philips

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:43.980
from CoreOS who talked about how
they use it internally as well.

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:44.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
and they use it a lot.

00:26:44.890 --> 00:26:46.181
MARK MANDEL: They use it a lot.

00:26:46.181 --> 00:26:47.920
So definitely well
worth the listen.

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:49.720
You can use protocol
buffers on their own,

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:52.159
but I think combined with gRPC
is just a really nice fit.

00:26:52.159 --> 00:26:53.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
and basically, gRPC,

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:55.866
what its going to do,
it's going to give you--

00:26:55.866 --> 00:26:58.480
so if protocol buffers
defines messages,

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:00.805
gRPC defines the
operations that receive

00:27:00.805 --> 00:27:02.200
and sends those messages.

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:02.800
Pretty much.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:03.180
MARK MANDEL: Pretty much.

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:04.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
a way of seeing it,

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:06.498
and since we're talking
about protocol buffers,

00:27:06.498 --> 00:27:08.260
there's also
another cool project

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:10.800
that I really like and I
discovered not that long ago.

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:12.204
It's called FlatBuffers.

00:27:12.204 --> 00:27:13.870
Of course you've heard
about FlatBuffers

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:16.779
because video game
designers love it.

00:27:16.779 --> 00:27:18.820
MARK MANDEL: Yes, I have
heard about FlatBuffers.

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:21.790
So FlatBuffers are literally
a slightly different way

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:24.250
of serializing data
up and down the pipe.

00:27:24.250 --> 00:27:27.460
It still has an Interface
Definition Language.

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:29.380
But the thing that
makes it different

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:33.129
is that the data can
be accessed directly

00:27:33.129 --> 00:27:35.420
without having to parse and
unpack it on the other end.

00:27:35.420 --> 00:27:36.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, so basically,

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:40.060
the way I see FlatBuffers
is that in protocol buffers,

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:42.670
you need to decode the whole
message to get any of the data

00:27:42.670 --> 00:27:43.280
inside.

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:45.214
In FlatBuffers, you
can just, whenever

00:27:45.214 --> 00:27:47.380
you need that piece of data,
you access it directly,

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:50.590
which means that you're
doing, like, lazy decoding,

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:52.090
and if you have a
lot of information

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:55.422
and you want to just start
the game now, you can do it.

00:27:55.422 --> 00:27:57.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so you
get different pros and cons

00:27:57.630 --> 00:28:01.536
on each one on which side you
get the performance hit on.

00:28:01.536 --> 00:28:02.160
They both work.

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:03.700
The cool thing is,
if you're using gRPC,

00:28:03.700 --> 00:28:05.870
you can use FlatBuffers and
you can use protocol buffers.

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:06.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:28:06.110 --> 00:28:07.276
MARK MANDEL: It's up to you.

